hey still  produce their own food they still  produce  their own energy they just don't use  sunlight they use chemicals chemicals  such as methane are involved in a fairly  complex chemical reaction and the  product what's created is energy for  them to use so  autotrophs whether they're  photoautotrophs or chemoautotrophs  autotrophs are often called producers  since they produce the energy of an  ecosystem here is a food chain and you  can see that the plants at the bottom  make energy for the insects and that  energy moves up and up and up and up and  up the food chain well what about the  heterotrophs  so the heterotrophs are kind of just the  opposite these are organisms that cannot  make their own energy through  photosynthesis or through chemo  synthesis so they find ways to get  energy in other means and so we're going  to go over the different kinds of  consumers here's a wolf as a great  example of a consumer you'll see another  example involving a wolf in a moment so  one of the one of the more cute adorable  herbivores rabbits are a great example  because they are called herbivores  because they only eat plant another kind  would be carnivores here we have some  wolves on a hunt and so carnivores like  wolves only eat other animals and then  there's the organisms that will eat  plants or in organisms that eat plants  or animals are called omnivores bears  bears are a great example  they'll eat fish they'll eat berries  they'll eat fruits they'll eat other  animals and so they're a great example  of omnivores humans humans are a great  example of omnivores is one and then  there's the other kind of heterotroph  that we don't usually consider these are  the decomposers when organisms die there  are nutrients still left in that  organisms body and decomposers will  obtain nutrients from dead organisms and  eventually cause the organism to decay  couple categories of decomposers the  first are the decompose is called the  detritivores and like the earthworm the  picture of the earthworm detritivores  ingest in  feed on on nutrients from dead organisms  however the other category of decompose  the other category of decomposes are  called saprophyte they don't ingest they  don't have mouths they don't have a  stomach but they absorb they release  enzymes and to whatever it is they're  growing and then they absorb those  nutrients directly into their cells and  these are called saprophyte smush rooms  and fungus or a great example of  saprophyte s-- so whether the organism  feeds directly or indirectly whether the  hetero trough feeds directly on plants  or indirectly for instance the Frog gets  its energy indirectly from the plant  because the Frog would eat the insect  which ate another insect which ate a  plant so all life directly or indirectly  relies on autotrophs for energy so  that's it that's really the end of this  video but please stay tuned I would like  to acknowledge some of the photographers  who made some of these images possible  next I would like to thank and  acknowledge some of the photographer's  that allowed the use of their images for  this video without their their graphics  and their visuals I don't think the  video would be hit as as intriguing so  thank you very much  you
 

